WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.147
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4487.

2
00:00:01.147 --> 00:00:02.294
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:02.294 --> 00:00:03.441
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Thursday, July twenty-third.

4
00:00:03.441 --> 00:00:04.206
It's 11:30.

5
00:00:04.206 --> 00:00:06.500
We are at Mr. Haki . . . M.D.

6
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:14.912
Haki Vincent's home here in Mossville off the Coach Williams Road, on the corner of Coach Williams Road and Old Spanish Trail.

7
00:00:14.912 --> 00:00:15.676
This is . . .

8
00:00:15.676 --> 00:00:17.588
<v HAKI VINCENT>Y'all don't mind if I . . .?

9
00:00:17.588 --> 00:00:20.647
<v ARSENEAULT>No. You can paint while we talk that's . . .

10
00:00:20.647 --> 00:00:21.029
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

11
00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:21.794
<v ARSENEAULT>Absolutely fine.

12
00:00:21.794 --> 00:00:26.000
<v JAMIE DIGILORMO>Do you mind if I took a couple of pictures?

13
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:26.672
<v VINCENT>Feel free.

14
00:00:26.672 --> 00:00:27.345
<v DIGILORMO>Thank you.

15
00:00:27.345 --> 00:00:28.017
<v VINCENT>You’re home.

16
00:00:28.017 --> 00:00:28.353
(Laughs)

17
00:00:28.353 --> 00:00:29.026
<v DIGILORMO>Thank you.

18
00:00:29.026 --> 00:00:30.034
I like that.

19
00:00:30.034 --> 00:00:33.060
<v ARSENEAULT>And we spoke . . . This is our second interview together.

20
00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:37.095
The first one was more of a twenty minute kind of just . . .

21
00:00:37.095 --> 00:00:37.431
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

22
00:00:37.431 --> 00:00:38.103
<v ARSENEAULT>Brief conversation.

23
00:00:38.103 --> 00:00:41.802
But we want to get a little more in depth today.

24
00:00:41.802 --> 00:00:55.586
And get some more thoughts about what it was like growing up and your opinions on things or your views on things or your memories of you know what it was like here in Mossville and growing up when you did.

25
00:00:55.586 --> 00:01:02.647
So the first thing we wanted to know is we asked you this last time but just want to make sure.

26
00:01:02.647 --> 00:01:05.000
When and where were you born?

27
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.611
<v VINCENT>I was raised in Washington , D.C.

28
00:01:08.611 --> 00:01:09.213
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

29
00:01:09.213 --> 00:01:12.824
<v VINCENT>I was born in Casablanca, Morocco.

30
00:01:12.824 --> 00:01:17.037
<v ARSENEAULT>Was your dad . . . Was he working there?

31
00:01:17.037 --> 00:01:19.444
Or why were you . . . ?

32
00:01:19.444 --> 00:01:23.657
<v VINCENT>He's been an entrepreneur all his life.

33
00:01:23.657 --> 00:01:25.463
Like my grandparents.

34
00:01:25.463 --> 00:01:30.278
My family have been entrepreneurs all their life.

35
00:01:30.278 --> 00:01:44.120
When they were so called free from slavery that's how we obtained this land, because they got a patent from President Grover Cleveland.

36
00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:55.556
And it's another president I don't recall his name right off but my great-grandfather got his from Grover Cleveland.

37
00:01:55.556 --> 00:02:00.972
And my grandfather got his from this other president.

38
00:02:00.972 --> 00:02:06.389
But they both got a nice portion of land.

39
00:02:06.389 --> 00:02:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What were your grandparents' names?

40
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:10.483
<v VINCENT>Eli.

41
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:12.897
Eli Senior and Eli Junior.

42
00:02:12.897 --> 00:02:14.345
<v ARSENEAULT>Their last names?

43
00:02:14.345 --> 00:02:14.828
<v VINCENT>Vincent.

44
00:02:14.828 --> 00:02:17.724
<v ARSENEAULT>And then on your mother's side?

45
00:02:17.724 --> 00:02:20.138
<v VINCENT>On my mother's side, Collins.

46
00:02:20.138 --> 00:02:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Were they . . . Did they live in Mossville?

47
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.692
<v VINCENT>No. They were out of Virginia.

48
00:02:27.692 --> 00:02:34.462
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, but your . . . Eli Senior and Eli Junior lived in Mossville?

49
00:02:34.462 --> 00:02:35.077
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

50
00:02:35.077 --> 00:02:37.538
So where was I?

51
00:02:37.538 --> 00:02:40.615
<v ARSENEAULT>They got some land from . . .

52
00:02:40.615 --> 00:02:41.846
<v VINCENT>Oh yeah.

53
00:02:41.846 --> 00:02:46.769
They got some land which is patent land.

54
00:02:46.769 --> 00:02:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

55
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:57.925
<v VINCENT>Both of them got a portion of patent land that they were homesteading during those days.

56
00:02:57.925 --> 00:03:05.989
So that's how we came about this land which is called Kampoff Springs.

57
00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:06.610
And . . .

58
00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:09.711
<v ARSENEAULT>Is this the original land?

59
00:03:09.711 --> 00:03:10.332
<v VINCENT>Yes.

60
00:03:10.332 --> 00:03:19.016
They used to have post office here, store, and different things like that here.

61
00:03:19.016 --> 00:03:25.840
They used to farm, timber work, all that kind of stuff.

62
00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.941
But they were always entrepreneurs.

63
00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:43.829
My father made history here because before he was out of his teens he built the roller coaster that the community was amazed over.

64
00:03:43.829 --> 00:03:53.134
That's how much land they had and what kind of creativity they were working with.

65
00:03:53.134 --> 00:03:54.374
You know?

66
00:03:54.374 --> 00:03:54.995
So . . .

67
00:03:54.995 --> 00:03:56.856
<v ARSENEAULT>When was that?

68
00:03:56.856 --> 00:04:08.021
<v VINCENT>Oh that's back in . . . Like I say he wasn't out of his teens at that time when he

69
00:04:08.021 --> 00:04:09.262
<c.p>built it.

70
00:04:09.262 --> 00:04:22.289
Because before he left here he was around in his twenties he built a house that was not a shotgun house.

71
00:04:22.289 --> 00:04:23.529
<v ARSENEAULT>It wasn't?

72
00:04:23.529 --> 00:04:28.492
<v VINCENT>No. It was you know a unique house.

73
00:04:28.492 --> 00:04:36.556
That's when he went to Washington and he went to school for construction.

74
00:04:36.556 --> 00:04:40.898
Building and that's what we were into.

75
00:04:40.898 --> 00:04:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>This is Eli Junior?

76
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:44.512
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

77
00:04:44.512 --> 00:04:46.562
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that your father?

78
00:04:46.562 --> 00:04:47.587
Eli Junior?

79
00:04:47.587 --> 00:04:48.099
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

80
00:04:48.099 --> 00:04:49.636
That was Junior.

81
00:04:49.636 --> 00:04:52.198
My father was Simon Vincent.

82
00:04:52.198 --> 00:04:54.248
Eli was still here.

83
00:04:54.248 --> 00:04:56.298
<v ARSENEAULT>Eli was his father?

84
00:04:56.298 --> 00:04:56.810
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

85
00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:58.860
<v ARSENEAULT>And then your great-grandfather . . .

86
00:04:58.860 --> 00:04:59.372
<v VINCENT>Right.

87
00:04:59.372 --> 00:05:00.909
<v ARSENEAULT>…is Eli Senior.

88
00:05:00.909 --> 00:05:01.421
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

89
00:05:01.421 --> 00:05:01.934
True.

90
00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:02.446
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

91
00:05:02.446 --> 00:05:05.521
Trying to get it all straight.

92
00:05:05.521 --> 00:05:16.793
<v VINCENT>I have to kind of search my mind sometimes because I was being taught about down here and I was the gofer.

93
00:05:16.793 --> 00:05:17.818
You know?

94
00:05:17.818 --> 00:05:19.355
They made me.

95
00:05:19.355 --> 00:05:29.603
Made sure I would have some history down here and we'd come down here and that's the way it went.

96
00:05:29.603 --> 00:05:34.215
But so in Washington that's what we were doing.

97
00:05:34.215 --> 00:05:35.240
Building houses.

98
00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:46.000
We built I don't know if y'all have checked it out or not but we built Deanwood, which is historical.

99
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.565
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it?

100
00:05:47.565 --> 00:05:48.087
<v VINCENT>Deanwood.

101
00:05:48.087 --> 00:05:48.609
<v ARSENEAULT>Deanwood.

102
00:05:48.609 --> 00:05:50.174
<v VINCENT>In Washington D.C.

103
00:05:50.174 --> 00:05:50.696
Deanwood.

104
00:05:50.696 --> 00:05:52.261
Far northeast Deanwood.

105
00:05:52.261 --> 00:05:52.783
<v ARSENEAULT>D-E-A-N-W-O-O-D?

106
00:05:52.783 --> 00:05:53.826
One word?

107
00:05:53.826 --> 00:05:54.348
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

108
00:05:54.348 --> 00:05:56.957
It was a black community.

109
00:05:56.957 --> 00:06:01.130
So that's where I was raised, you know.

110
00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:02.174
In a

111
00:06:02.174 --> 00:06:03.217
<c.p>black community.

112
00:06:03.217 --> 00:06:09.478
And only time we saw white faces was when we'd go downtown.

113
00:06:09.478 --> 00:06:11.043
Something like that.

114
00:06:11.043 --> 00:06:14.696
Or maybe someone come through the community.

115
00:06:14.696 --> 00:06:28.783
But so at that time they had an amusement park called Glen Echo in Washington D. C. And that's where everybody used to go to be amused.

116
00:06:28.783 --> 00:06:29.826
You know?

117
00:06:29.826 --> 00:06:31.391
Ride and whatnot.

118
00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:35.043
But they wouldn't let blacks go there.

119
00:06:35.043 --> 00:06:47.565
So we built one in Deanwood because we had a street car used to come from downtown Washington all the way out to Maryland.

120
00:06:47.565 --> 00:06:50.174
Turn around and go back.

121
00:06:50.174 --> 00:06:54.348
So we built this you know amusement park.

122
00:06:54.348 --> 00:07:00.609
Now I got to think of the name of it [Suburban Gardens].

123
00:07:00.609 --> 00:07:05.304
But it's all in the paper work on Deanwood.

124
00:07:05.304 --> 00:07:07.391
The making of Deanwood.

125
00:07:07.391 --> 00:07:10.522
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, we can look that up.

126
00:07:10.522 --> 00:07:12.609
<v VINCENT>The history of it.

127
00:07:12.609 --> 00:07:13.130
Yeah.

128
00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:13.652
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

129
00:07:13.652 --> 00:07:18.348
So how did your father end up in Africa?

130
00:07:18.348 --> 00:07:19.391
In Casablanca?

131
00:07:19.391 --> 00:07:20.957
What's that story?

132
00:07:20.957 --> 00:07:22.000
<v VINCENT>[Pardon]?

133
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.573
<v ARSENEAULT>What's that story?

134
00:07:23.573 --> 00:07:26.195
Or was it your mother?

135
00:07:26.195 --> 00:07:33.012
I don't mean to assume that . . . . It was your father and your mother.

136
00:07:33.012 --> 00:07:44.549
<v VINCENT>Yeah, but a lot of that history is . . . evades me because I didn't learn . . . I was learning more about being the gofer.

137
00:07:44.549 --> 00:07:45.598
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

138
00:07:45.598 --> 00:07:53.988
<v VINCENT>Learning the trade because he was very strict on you know us learning to be entrepreneurs.

139
00:07:53.988 --> 00:07:58.183
That was our key lesson in coming up.

140
00:07:58.183 --> 00:08:02.378
<v ARSENEAULT>When you say “we,” was it your siblings?

141
00:08:02.378 --> 00:08:02.902
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

142
00:08:02.902 --> 00:08:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How many siblings?

143
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.280
<v VINCENT>Three sisters and let me see . . . Three sister and three brothers.

144
00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:10.760
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

145
00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:12.680
Were you the oldest?

146
00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:17.960
<v VINCENT>No. I got a sister who's still living is the oldest.

147
00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:24.680
And I got a half-brother who was around her age who has passed away.

148
00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.520
Matter of fact, he brought this car here.

149
00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:40.040
It was pristine when he came with it but he gave it to somebody to sell for me and that's what happened to it.

150
00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:46.760
But he was a half-brother and he was the exact image of my father.

151
00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:58.760
Because one day I came home, that's before this road was through here and we had a little gravel road coming around to the gate.

152
00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:04.040
And I came in and I saw him standing up there.

153
00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:07.400
I had . . . It was like deja vu.

154
00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:13.160
Like my father had came back and was standing outside my gate.

155
00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:14.120
My half-brother.

156
00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:14.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

157
00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.960
<v VINCENT>It blew my mind, I'm telling you.

158
00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:20.840
But he was a dental technician.

159
00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:27.560
He was going to teach me that, but he passed away before he could.

160
00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:29.480
Before I could learn.

161
00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:41.000
So alright I was . . . When I was . . . My worst experience coming up was I got robbed by two white cops when I was sixteen.

162
00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:48.680
I was driving, going you know to see some honey somewhere and they stopped me and . . .

163
00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:53.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh you were going to see a lady?

164
00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:53.393
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

165
00:09:53.393 --> 00:09:53.787
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

166
00:09:53.787 --> 00:09:58.113
<v VINCENT>And they stopped me and asked me for my driver's license.

167
00:09:58.113 --> 00:10:05.193
I gave it to them and they went around a couple of corners and they said, "Follow us."

168
00:10:05.193 --> 00:10:09.520
They went around a couple of corners in a dark area.

169
00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:14.240
Came back to the car asked me, "How much money you got?"

170
00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:21.320
So whatever I had, you know, I took it out and you know, "This how much I got."

171
00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:23.287
They took what they wanted.

172
00:10:23.287 --> 00:10:24.467
<v DIGILORMO>Where was this?

173
00:10:24.467 --> 00:10:25.253
<v VINCENT>Washington, D.C.

174
00:10:25.253 --> 00:10:25.647
Deanwood.

175
00:10:25.647 --> 00:10:29.580
They took what they wanted and told me, "Get out

176
00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:30.367
<c.p>of here.

177
00:10:30.367 --> 00:10:33.513
We don't want to see you no more."

178
00:10:33.513 --> 00:10:34.300
You know?

179
00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:40.987
So for over twenty years I didn't even tell anybody about it because I was in shock.

180
00:10:40.987 --> 00:10:43.740
And I knew wouldn't nobody believe me.

181
00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:44.527
You know?

182
00:10:44.527 --> 00:10:45.313
So I . . .

183
00:10:45.313 --> 00:10:47.673
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that sort of thing common?

184
00:10:47.673 --> 00:10:48.067
<v VINCENT>[Pardon]?

185
00:10:48.067 --> 00:10:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that sort of thing common for that time?

186
00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:52.893
<v VINCENT>Not really.

187
00:10:52.893 --> 00:10:58.695
Not that I knew of anyway, because I was mostly home and working.

188
00:10:58.695 --> 00:11:09.407
But as far as being out in the street and what not, you know, it was really like country, wasn’t out in no street.

189
00:11:09.407 --> 00:11:10.299
You know?

190
00:11:10.299 --> 00:11:12.085
It was like here.

191
00:11:12.085 --> 00:11:16.994
People had chickens and all that stuff right there in Washington.

192
00:11:16.994 --> 00:11:20.565
Chickens, horses, gardens, and all that good stuff.

193
00:11:20.565 --> 00:11:27.706
So that was, I guess that's a part of what made me what I am today.

194
00:11:27.706 --> 00:11:29.045
That one experience.

195
00:11:29.045 --> 00:11:38.418
Especially with two white faces and you know you don't see them in Deanwood at that time too much at all.

196
00:11:38.418 --> 00:11:48.684
So I was raised hard like I could . . . I figured out later on that this is the way I was treated being raised.

197
00:11:48.684 --> 00:11:56.718
Like when we got . . . When we did something wrong like we'd go down to the creek and swim.

198
00:11:56.718 --> 00:11:59.395
We not supposed to do that.

199
00:11:59.395 --> 00:12:06.537
And they find out you did it and your daddy come home you got a whipping.

200
00:12:06.537 --> 00:12:11.446
And you going to get one before he come home, too.

201
00:12:11.446 --> 00:12:15.017
And you got to take your clothes off.

202
00:12:15.017 --> 00:12:23.051
I didn't, you know I didn't put it together then but that's nothing but an offshoot of slavery.

203
00:12:23.051 --> 00:12:23.944
You know?

204
00:12:23.944 --> 00:12:26.175
That's what they were taught.

205
00:12:26.175 --> 00:12:28.407
This is how you doing.

206
00:12:28.407 --> 00:12:33.316
This is how you break them and raise them and stuff.

207
00:12:33.316 --> 00:12:35.548
To me that was torture.

208
00:12:35.548 --> 00:12:38.226
You got to go out and

209
00:12:38.226 --> 00:12:49.831
<c.p>break you a switch off and it can't get no roody-poo. [Laughs] It's got to be you know something that's going to stand up under pressure.

210
00:12:49.831 --> 00:12:58.757
You got to break that off clean the leaves off and whatnot, bring it in and wait for your whipping.

211
00:12:58.757 --> 00:13:00.542
What that sound like?

212
00:13:00.542 --> 00:13:02.774
That ever happen to you?

213
00:13:02.774 --> 00:13:10.362
So later on as I you know grew wiser I see where it came from, you know.

214
00:13:10.362 --> 00:13:13.486
I said, "Well this is nothing nice."

215
00:13:13.486 --> 00:13:20.181
And you know, I also discovered around that time it's economic exploitation under white supremacy.

216
00:13:20.181 --> 00:13:24.644
I had to figure that out you know coming out.

217
00:13:24.644 --> 00:13:25.537
So I . . .

218
00:13:25.537 --> 00:13:30.000
<v DIGILORMO>What experiences opened your eyes to that economic exploitation?

219
00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.625
<v VINCENT>When I . . . When they threatened us to be drafted.

220
00:13:33.625 --> 00:13:36.847
That put a bad taste in my mouth.

221
00:13:36.847 --> 00:13:41.681
You going to force somebody to go in and fight for what?

222
00:13:41.681 --> 00:13:42.486
For who?

223
00:13:42.486 --> 00:13:42.889
How?

224
00:13:42.889 --> 00:13:47.722
All of that stuff that's got to go through your head, too.

225
00:13:47.722 --> 00:13:48.528
And why?

226
00:13:48.528 --> 00:13:56.181
Because I was already working for them because coming up in school we had to collect newspapers and whatnot.

227
00:13:56.181 --> 00:13:58.597
That was World War II now.

228
00:13:58.597 --> 00:14:03.028
We had to collect newspapers and stuff to help the war.

229
00:14:03.028 --> 00:14:07.458
Whatever they going to use the papers for, I don't know.

230
00:14:07.458 --> 00:14:12.292
But you know, so we collected enough newspapers to buy a jeep.

231
00:14:12.292 --> 00:14:14.708
So our school bought a jeep.

232
00:14:14.708 --> 00:14:19.139
They brought it to the school and let us see it.

233
00:14:19.139 --> 00:14:21.556
Go up to [ . . .?] here and whatnot.

234
00:14:21.556 --> 00:14:22.764
I said, "Oh."

235
00:14:22.764 --> 00:14:24.778
<v ARSENEAULT>This is in Washington, D.C.?

236
00:14:24.778 --> 00:14:28.000
Did you go to public school there?

237
00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:28.493
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

238
00:14:28.493 --> 00:14:29.973
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it segregated?

239
00:14:29.973 --> 00:14:31.945
<v VINCENT>It was all black.

240
00:14:31.945 --> 00:14:38.849
But you can't say it wasn't segregated because what they were teaching, you know?

241
00:14:38.849 --> 00:14:40.329
Was not altogether.

242
00:14:40.329 --> 00:14:42.301
I could see that.

243
00:14:42.301 --> 00:14:53.644
That's why . . . That's when I figured out, damn they got me in here teaching me not how to think but what to think.

244
00:14:53.644 --> 00:14:56.110
This ain’t working for me.

245
00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:58.575
So my computer shut down.

246
00:14:58.575 --> 00:15:01.534
I didn't learn nothing in school.

247
00:15:01.534 --> 00:15:02.027
Zero.

248
00:15:02.027 --> 00:15:05.479
I couldn’t pay attention to that bullshit.

249
00:15:05.479 --> 00:15:08.438
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about school?

250
00:15:08.438 --> 00:15:11.890
What else do you remember about school?

251
00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:16.822
<v VINCENT>Oh that's another place they used to whip you, too.

252
00:15:16.822 --> 00:15:17.808
In school.

253
00:15:17.808 --> 00:15:18.301
True.

254
00:15:18.301 --> 00:15:22.247
Take a ruler to your hand and whatever.

255
00:15:22.247 --> 00:15:23.233
You know?

256
00:15:23.233 --> 00:15:27.671
They had different methods to doing you there too.

257
00:15:27.671 --> 00:15:31.123
But one thing was no white teachers.

258
00:15:31.123 --> 00:15:32.603
No white students.

259
00:15:32.603 --> 00:15:34.575
It was all black.

260
00:15:34.575 --> 00:15:35.562
Used books.

261
00:15:35.562 --> 00:15:36.548
Pages missing.

262
00:15:36.548 --> 00:15:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Are they from the white schools?

263
00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:40.921
<v VINCENT>Leftover shit.

264
00:15:40.921 --> 00:15:43.223
That's what we would get.

265
00:15:43.223 --> 00:15:47.366
So how you really going to learn something anyhow?

266
00:15:47.366 --> 00:15:48.287
You know?

267
00:15:48.287 --> 00:15:51.050
You don't have all the tools.

268
00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:54.272
You missed some of the major tools.

269
00:15:54.272 --> 00:16:00.257
So I can see that as being set up to be economic exploitation.

270
00:16:00.257 --> 00:16:05.322
Make the rich richer while we stay right where we are.

271
00:16:05.322 --> 00:16:18.673
So I think my wisdom must came from my ancestors because when I came down here a lot of old people who are gone now used to tell me,

272
00:16:18.673 --> 00:16:20.975
"You just like your grandfather.

273
00:16:20.975 --> 00:16:26.960
Your grandfather Eli used to ride a white horse and carry a whip.

274
00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:34.787
He would give you the shirt off his back, the food out of his garden and stuff.

275
00:16:34.787 --> 00:16:37.089
But don't screw him around.

276
00:16:37.089 --> 00:16:39.851
That's why he got that whip."

277
00:16:39.851 --> 00:16:41.233
Black or white.

278
00:16:41.233 --> 00:16:43.074
Didn't make no difference.

279
00:16:43.074 --> 00:16:44.916
So I thank God.

280
00:16:44.916 --> 00:16:45.376
You

281
00:16:45.376 --> 00:16:45.837
<c.p>know?

282
00:16:45.837 --> 00:16:53.663
That's one thing that made me realize in school that they are teaching us what to think.

283
00:16:53.663 --> 00:16:56.426
We never learned how to think.

284
00:16:56.426 --> 00:17:00.569
So the military really started, you know that threat.

285
00:17:00.569 --> 00:17:06.094
That bullyness of you got to go and do what we say.

286
00:17:06.094 --> 00:17:08.396
<v ARSENEAULT>You said you were seventeen . . .

287
00:17:08.396 --> 00:17:08.856
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

288
00:17:08.856 --> 00:17:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . when you decided to join the air force?

289
00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:13.304
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

290
00:17:13.304 --> 00:17:22.118
And the reason I joined the air force is because I didn't want to go in the army and be shooting and all that grunt stuff and you know.

291
00:17:22.118 --> 00:17:26.069
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have to do something special to get in the air force?

292
00:17:26.069 --> 00:17:28.804
<v VINCENT>Same thing that all the rest of them do.

293
00:17:28.804 --> 00:17:30.020
They just want bodies.

294
00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:30.627
That they . . .

295
00:17:30.627 --> 00:17:33.059
<v ARSENEAULT>You didn't have to be in ROTC beforehand?

296
00:17:33.059 --> 00:17:33.363
<v VINCENT>No.

297
00:17:33.363 --> 00:17:35.490
<v ARSENEAULT>Where you ever in ROTC or anything?

298
00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:41.873
<v VINCENT>No. Only thing I was in before that was the Boy Scouts and I learned a lot from the boy scouts.

299
00:17:41.873 --> 00:17:44.000
Far as life and camping whatnot.

300
00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:46.065
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that segregated, too?

301
00:17:46.065 --> 00:17:48.647
<v VINCENT>No it was all black.

302
00:17:48.647 --> 00:17:50.712
Deanwood was all black.

303
00:17:50.712 --> 00:17:53.293
We had a black movie.

304
00:17:53.293 --> 00:17:55.875
We had an amusement park.

305
00:17:55.875 --> 00:18:02.071
You know, we could ride the street cars to town and back.

306
00:18:02.071 --> 00:18:03.103
You know?

307
00:18:03.103 --> 00:18:07.750
But you didn't see no white faces out there.

308
00:18:07.750 --> 00:18:17.043
Then they started building . . . We were like in the country and the started building projects there around us.

309
00:18:17.043 --> 00:18:20.658
You know, they were building houses, too.

310
00:18:20.658 --> 00:18:26.337
But started building several projects and things started really changing then.

311
00:18:26.337 --> 00:18:33.049
Start seeing more white cops riding through the community and all of this.

312
00:18:33.049 --> 00:18:42.342
So they were boosting up the exploitation and the entire . . . They ask people why they run from cops.

313
00:18:42.342 --> 00:18:45.957
That sister is probably the same way.

314
00:18:45.957 --> 00:18:46.989
She terrified.

315
00:18:46.989 --> 00:18:51.120
He got a gun and you the victim.

316
00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:54.734
How else can you not be terrified?

317
00:18:54.734 --> 00:18:59.897
But so the military was another trip that wasn't right.

318
00:18:59.897 --> 00:19:09.707
And what they do is get you in there and terrorize you let you know that you ain't shit.

319
00:19:09.707 --> 00:19:14.870
We going to make you over so you'll be right.

320
00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about boot camp?

321
00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:20.308
<v VINCENT>This is it.

322
00:19:20.308 --> 00:19:21.617
<v ARSENEAULT>This is the . . .?

323
00:19:21.617 --> 00:19:22.925
<v VINCENT>You ain’t shit.

324
00:19:22.925 --> 00:19:24.233
You ain’t nothing.

325
00:19:24.233 --> 00:19:27.722
<v ARSENEAULT>That's what they told you in boot camp?

326
00:19:27.722 --> 00:19:29.030
<v VINCENT>You ain’t nobody.

327
00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:32.519
We going to make somebody out of you.

328
00:19:32.519 --> 00:19:33.391
You know?

329
00:19:33.391 --> 00:19:42.113
Because we need this certain tool and we going to make you this certain tool that we need to kill.

330
00:19:42.113 --> 00:19:49.962
So that’s when I was on the train on the way to boot camp that's when I noticed.

331
00:19:49.962 --> 00:19:55.195
I ain’t never seen so many white faces in my life together.

332
00:19:55.195 --> 00:19:56.068
You know?

333
00:19:56.068 --> 00:19:57.376
On the train.

334
00:19:57.376 --> 00:20:03.481
So I said, "Uh oh, this is going to be a rough road here."

335
00:20:03.481 --> 00:20:04.353
For real.

336
00:20:04.353 --> 00:20:09.150
So at the same time now I'm reading about Emmett Till.

337
00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:10.895
Y'all know Emmett Till?

338
00:20:10.895 --> 00:20:12.203
<v DIGILORMO>I think so.

339
00:20:12.203 --> 00:20:13.511
<v ARSENEAULT>I might know.

340
00:20:13.511 --> 00:20:17.000
I'll have her educate me on it.

341
00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:17.486
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

342
00:20:17.486 --> 00:20:22.838
<v DIGILORMO>He was a young man killed in Mississippi during the sixties.

343
00:20:22.838 --> 00:20:27.216
<v VINCENT>He left Chicago to visit his family in Mississippi.

344
00:20:27.216 --> 00:20:30.622
He ended up in the Mississippi River.

345
00:20:30.622 --> 00:20:34.514
A big iron wheel tied around his neck.

346
00:20:34.514 --> 00:20:35.486
<v DIGILORMO>Like fifteen.

347
00:20:35.486 --> 00:20:42.784
<v VINCENT>Yeah about fifteen years old and the reason they said they did it because he

348
00:20:42.784 --> 00:20:45.216
<c.p>whistled at a white girl.

349
00:20:45.216 --> 00:20:49.595
But the people just . . . They just went to court.

350
00:20:49.595 --> 00:20:52.514
I'm concerned it's not long ago.

351
00:20:52.514 --> 00:20:56.892
The trial came up, it was not long ago.

352
00:20:56.892 --> 00:20:59.324
That's how long, you know.

353
00:20:59.324 --> 00:21:00.297
<v ARSENEAULT>It took.

354
00:21:00.297 --> 00:21:07.108
I'm curious what did they tell you about the Korean conflict before you enlisted?

355
00:21:07.108 --> 00:21:11.000
Like what did you know about it?

356
00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:11.406
<v VINCENT>Nothing.

357
00:21:11.406 --> 00:21:14.650
<v ARSENEAULT>They didn't inform y'all what it was about?

358
00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:16.678
<v VINCENT>Other than it's a war.

359
00:21:16.678 --> 00:21:19.517
The Japanese, the Germans, the you know.

360
00:21:19.517 --> 00:21:22.761
We trying to keep them from . . . Oh yeah,

361
00:21:22.761 --> 00:21:27.222
we used to have air raid war . . . Air raid . . . What did . . .

362
00:21:27.222 --> 00:21:27.628
<v DIGILORMO>Drills?

363
00:21:27.628 --> 00:21:28.033
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

364
00:21:28.033 --> 00:21:39.794
Where they . . . Matter of fact I got a . . . I got one of the helmets right over there on that green chair right now that we . . . they used to wear.

365
00:21:39.794 --> 00:22:00.883
They put a metal helmet on that was a certain color and they'd go around the community making sure everybody got their lights out and everybody in the house because all of the lights in the city going to turn out in case the enemy coming they can't see where we are.

366
00:22:00.883 --> 00:22:01.289
Really.

367
00:22:01.289 --> 00:22:02.911
<v ARSENEAULT>So y'all would practice?

368
00:22:02.911 --> 00:22:03.317
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

369
00:22:03.317 --> 00:22:10.211
They . . . When the siren ring everybody had to turn their lights out and go in the house.

370
00:22:10.211 --> 00:22:11.833
<v ARSENEAULT>Where would y'all go?

371
00:22:11.833 --> 00:22:14.267
Y'all . . . Was there a basement or . . .?

372
00:22:14.267 --> 00:22:17.917
<v VINCENT>We had an air raid shelter in our house.

373
00:22:17.917 --> 00:22:18.728
We would . . .

374
00:22:18.728 --> 00:22:20.756
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your dad build it?

375
00:22:20.756 --> 00:22:21.972
<v VINCENT>We built it.

376
00:22:21.972 --> 00:22:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Your family built it?

377
00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:24.296
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

378
00:22:24.296 --> 00:22:25.479
So we had that.

379
00:22:25.479 --> 00:22:30.211
And a lot of people didn't, you know, they just had the house and that's it.

380
00:22:30.211 --> 00:22:33.761
But that was one of the . . . trips we used to go through.

381
00:22:33.761 --> 00:22:35.831
<v ARSENEAULT>How did your dad learn these skills?

382
00:22:35.831 --> 00:22:37.606
I'm sorry I'm just curious about . . .

383
00:22:37.606 --> 00:22:38.789
<v VINCENT>He went to school.

384
00:22:38.789 --> 00:22:42.930
<v ARSENEAULT>So he went to a trade school here or he went to trade school . . .?

385
00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:44.113
<v VINCENT>No, in Washington, D.C.

386
00:22:44.113 --> 00:22:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>In D.C.?

387
00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:46.237
<v VINCENT>Matter of fact . . .

388
00:22:46.237 --> 00:22:51.186
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he talk about your uncle . . . your grandfather while y'all were there?

389
00:22:51.186 --> 00:23:01.082
<v VINCENT>Yeah he used to tell me all the time about the main thing he would be talking about is the land we had here.

390
00:23:01.082 --> 00:23:05.206
That's the main subject and learning to be an entrepreneur.

391
00:23:05.206 --> 00:23:09.742
Those are two key things that came up in our family.

392
00:23:09.742 --> 00:23:15.928
We didn't get a lot of hugs and kisses and love and stuff like that.

393
00:23:15.928 --> 00:23:19.639
It was tough love that we was raised with.

394
00:23:19.639 --> 00:23:20.464
You know?

395
00:23:20.464 --> 00:23:22.526
<v ARSENEAULT>Was your mom from here?

396
00:23:22.526 --> 00:23:25.000
<v VINCENT>No, she was from Virginia.

397
00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:27.800
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your dad meet her . . .?

398
00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:28.920
<v VINCENT>Richmond area.

399
00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:31.720
He met her in Washington.

400
00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:32.840
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

401
00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:39.000
<v VINCENT>So we wasn't . . . We couldn't run around the street and whatnot.

402
00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:44.040
We get in trouble for going in people's yard.

403
00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:49.080
Picking apples and pears off the tree without asking.

404
00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:49.640
Stealing.

405
00:23:49.640 --> 00:24:01.960
<c.p>[Laughs] But coming up that's the only kind of, you know, trouble we would get in about breaking the rules at home.

406
00:24:01.960 --> 00:24:07.000
<v DIGILORMO>So you weren't in Morocco very long then?

407
00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:07.323
<v VINCENT>No.

408
00:24:07.323 --> 00:24:09.581
<v DIGILORMO>And you grew up in D.C. mostly?

409
00:24:09.581 --> 00:24:12.484
<v VINCENT>No. I was very young when I came here.

410
00:24:12.484 --> 00:24:13.452
<v DIGILORMO>So you don't . . .

411
00:24:13.452 --> 00:24:15.065
<v VINCENT>That's why I don't have . . .

412
00:24:15.065 --> 00:24:16.677
<v DIGILORMO>Many memories of that place?

413
00:24:16.677 --> 00:24:18.935
<v VINCENT>Yeah, but I have been back, though.

414
00:24:18.935 --> 00:24:19.903
<v DIGILORMO>Oh you have?

415
00:24:19.903 --> 00:24:20.226
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

416
00:24:20.226 --> 00:24:21.839
Two time, matter of fact.

417
00:24:21.839 --> 00:24:26.032
One time when I was in the military because I was in SAC.

418
00:24:26.032 --> 00:24:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What's that?

419
00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:28.335
<v VINCENT>Strategic Air Command.

420
00:24:28.335 --> 00:24:31.897
Supposed to be the backbone of American defense.

421
00:24:31.897 --> 00:24:35.013
So we traveled everywhere all the time.

422
00:24:35.013 --> 00:24:45.697
So the first, one of the first major stops we made . . . We had re-fuelers and bombers and all that kind of stuff you know.

423
00:24:45.697 --> 00:24:50.594
And when we go on missions all of that be together.

424
00:24:50.594 --> 00:24:55.490
Refueling planes in the air and bombers and all that stuff.

425
00:24:55.490 --> 00:25:03.948
One of the first places we stopped stayed for let me see, I don't know how long, but England.

426
00:25:03.948 --> 00:25:08.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Was this the first time being out of the country?

427
00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:12.406
<v VINCENT>No, I was in other little islands and whatever.

428
00:25:12.406 --> 00:25:16.858
We stopped on the way places you know, but yeah.

429
00:25:16.858 --> 00:25:19.084
So England was a trip.

430
00:25:19.084 --> 00:25:19.529
England . . .

431
00:25:19.529 --> 00:25:21.310
<v ARSENEAULT>All the white people?

432
00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:21.755
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

433
00:25:21.755 --> 00:25:25.761
It was a lot of Africans there, too, though.

434
00:25:25.761 --> 00:25:30.213
But England remind me of . . . Boston remind me of England.

435
00:25:30.213 --> 00:25:34.665
I did not like England, I do not like Boston.

436
00:25:34.665 --> 00:25:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why not?

437
00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:37.755
<v VINCENT>It looked Euro.

438
00:25:37.755 --> 00:25:38.926
That's why.

439
00:25:38.926 --> 00:25:40.096
Feel Euro.

440
00:25:40.096 --> 00:25:43.021
Cold, wet all that stuff.

441
00:25:43.021 --> 00:25:43.606
<v DIGILORMO>Scary?

442
00:25:43.606 --> 00:25:44.191
<v ARSENEAULT>Bricks?

443
00:25:44.191 --> 00:25:44.777
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

444
00:25:44.777 --> 00:25:46.532
Old like ancient.

445
00:25:46.532 --> 00:25:51.798
No. I'm downtown in London, walking, looking you know.

446
00:25:51.798 --> 00:25:57.649
Next thing I know this white girl holding my arm.

447
00:25:57.649 --> 00:26:02.330
Asking me . . . What was the word she used?

448
00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:05.840
Anyway, she was a trick [prostitute].

449
00:26:05.840 --> 00:26:12.862
<v DIGILORMO>[Laughs] She was asking if you wanted to have a good time?

450
00:26:12.862 --> 00:26:13.447
<v VINCENT>Yeah.

451
00:26:13.447 --> 00:26:16.957
I said, "I don't think so.

452
00:26:16.957 --> 00:26:20.468
I'm already having a good time."

453
00:26:20.468 --> 00:26:27.489
It's the first time I ever touched a white girl, you know.

454
00:26:27.489 --> 00:26:29.830
White girl touched me.

455
00:26:29.830 --> 00:26:31.000
<v DIGILORMO>Right.

456
00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:33.500
<v VINCENT>So I said, "Uh uh.

457
00:26:33.500 --> 00:26:35.500
I got a wife.

458
00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:40.000
This might . . . Ain't no telling what's up with this."

459
00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:45.000
So she said, "Well, I ain't going to charge you.

460
00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:46.500
I like you."

461
00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:48.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Got a special deal.

462
00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:50.500
<v VINCENT>Yeah. [Laughs] So say.

463
00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:53.000
But that's what she was.

464
00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:56.000
She was trying to get me.

465
00:26:56.000 --> 00:27:00.000
So I said, "Well I ain't doing nothing.

466
00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.000
I guess I'll hang out with you some."

467
00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:10.500
Biggest dose of crabs. [Laughs] And I ain't never had nothing like that.

468
00:27:10.500 --> 00:27:14.000
I didn't even know what they were.

469
00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:15.500
<v DIGILORMO>Off the record.

470
00:27:15.500 --> 00:27:17.500
Off the record. [Laughter]

471
00:27:17.500 --> 00:27:18.500
<v VINCENT>Well y'all . . .

472
00:27:18.500 --> 00:27:22.000
